A||||6||Brandon86SE||Horrible squealing||||5||
Z||000000||Brandon86SE||08-29-2001||12:39 AM||||Alright, here's my problem.  I have a squealing sound coming from the back of the car when I'm driving.  It is pretty much constantly there as long as the car is running, except for when I have the brakes pushed down.  That seems really weird to me, 'cause typically I'd expect a squealing to mean something's up with the fan belt.  I'm not sure whether to look at brakes, or the belt.  I just had the front brakes done, and wanted the rears done too, but the mechanic said I still had a year left on the pads, so recommended leaving them.  The thing that really makes me think fan belt is that twice now (over a period of about a month) I've started up the car and got a loud, horrible squeal.  One time I thought it wasn't me, and it went away as soon as I backed out of the driveway, the second time I knew it was me, so I stopped the car, started it again, and it was fine.  But anyway, the squeal is persistent when I'm driving.  If anyone has any ideas it'd be much appreciated.  Thanks!<P>Brandon||||reg||5
Z||000001||rodmcneill||08-29-2001||01:02 AM||||Baby pig?||||reg||12
Z||000002||MrPBody||08-29-2001||07:09 AM||||Yep, those sound like symptoms of a loose belt.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Kasdan||08-29-2001||08:54 AM||||Did i a box of kittens get wedged in your engine compartment?  Sorry if that sounds cruel, i thought we all needed a little comedic relief.  Anywho, tighten the Alternator belt, that was my problem.<P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan<BR>1986 GT Fastback, <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FIERO2.JPG">       <BR>Holley Ram-Air, Power Steering, K&N Filtercharger, Bosch Wires, Bosch Platinum Plugs, Conrad Distribuiter Cap/Rotor, New GT Seats, New Headliner, New Carpet, Ocelet Exhaust, Red Underbody Light Kit (coming soon)<BR>Aim=FieroDriver1986||||reg||1
Z||000004||84Bill||08-29-2001||09:22 AM||||Could be the wear indicators on the brake pads! Pull the wheels off the front and rear on the side the noise is coming from.||||reg||4
Z||000005||starfighter007@msn.com||08-29-2001||11:08 AM||||when you start the car and you hear the squeel and your not movig that should tell you that its not the brakes . And I agree with Kasdan it is most likely your alt. belt that is loose||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ed||08-29-2001||08:47 PM||||Car stopped and squealing, something motor related.<P>Rolling, it could be the wear indicator on the brakes.  I found that the brakes stopped squealing when I applied them. <P>A "mechanics stethoscope" (or a long screwdriver) can be used to check bearings in your alternator or water pump.  If you have air conditioning that belt can be slipping, or the compressor could be failing.  <P> ||||reg||1
